## Proof

The group $\mathbb{Z}^*_p$ (the multiplicative group of integers modulo $p$) is well known to be cyclic of order $p - 1$.

A fundamental property of cyclic groups is:

> **Fact.** A cyclic group of order $n$ contains an element of order $d$ for every positive divisor $d$ of $n$.

Indeed, if $g$ is a generator of the cyclic group $G$ of order $n$, then $g^{n/d}$ has order $d$ whenever $d \mid n$.

We apply this with $n = p - 1$ and $d = (p-1)/2$.

Since $p$ is a (large) prime, $p$ is odd and $p > 2$, so $p - 1$ is even and $(p-1)/2$ is a positive integer. Clearly

$$\frac{p-1}{2} \mid (p-1),$$

so $(p-1)/2$ is a divisor of the group order $p - 1$.

By the fact above, the cyclic group $\mathbb{Z}^*_p$ of order $p - 1$ contains an element of order $(p-1)/2$. Explicitly, if $g$ is any primitive root modulo $p$, then

$$g^{(p-1)/\,((p-1)/2)} = g^{2}$$

has order $(p-1)/2$.

Therefore, **yes**, there exists an element of order $(p-1)/2$ in $\mathbb{Z}^*_p$. $\blacksquare$

